WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Touchdown Fever!

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.060
It's the muscle, the hustle, the chomp.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:30.500
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.580
I'm Mike.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.660
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.260
Guys, it really is the muscle, the hustle, and the chomp of the NFL.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.540
It's that time of the year again.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:48.520
Obviously, recording this in real time, the Buffalo Bills remain undefeated.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.480
Don't you put that evil on us.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.720
Don't make that happen.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.820
Don't make it. Yeah, don't let them be undefeated.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.200
Let them lose a game or two.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.180
I just, you know, I think you actually made them lose.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.000
If we lost last week.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.480
Probably in the new stadium.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.300
No way. I've been hearing some shaky things about the new stadium recently.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.460
Yeah, they've got some obstructed views.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.100
They've got some...

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.100
Don't worry. The obstructed views will only be sold for concerts.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.500
Yeah, yeah. They say that.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:18.980
Yeah, that's reassuring.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.160
And then what's going on with the wind tunnels?

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.960
What about the wind tunnels?

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.760
I heard that they're pretty extreme.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.640
Huh. That sounds cool.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.360
That sounds sick. Yeah, I like wind tunnels.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.160
Yeah, that sounds awesome. Right. That's like a perk.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.420
Yeah, yeah. It's a Buffalo living simulator.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.160
Yeah. They call us the Windy City.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:39.580
Yeah. I did.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.320
I know that they don't call you guys that, but I did notice when I was in Buffalo for my

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.960
four years in college, it was always the windiest right before I got back to my dorm room.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.880
Yeah, that's kind of a common weather thing for...

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.640
It was like right before I had to get to any particular door that was going to bring me

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.460
to warmth, it got significantly windier.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.160
I think it's just literally that the campus is a wind tunnel in itself.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.100
We're getting off track here.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.140
I don't think we're getting off track, actually, because I was just about to ask you.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.020
September 13th was the Bills' first game.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.120
It was not a home game, so they didn't play at the new stadium.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.960
They played against the Texans.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.100
Sean, do you remember the score of that game?

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.180
The score of the Texans game?

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.560
Yeah. Do you remember?

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.480
I mean, I know it was like eight days ago.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.140
Okay. Yeah, I can do this.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.500
Do you want me to predict the previous game before...

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.300
No, I just want you to tell me what the score was.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.540
Yeah, not predict, just remember. Yeah, just tell me what it was.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.040
Because it already happened. It was 28 to 17 Bills.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:54.820
Right. And now, you had a prop bet on it for Josh Allen over under two touchdowns.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.800
Do you remember?

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.640
If I won that?

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.840
Yeah. Yes, I definitely won that.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.720
Especially if the score was 28, yeah.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.480
Yes, you did.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.980
Now, here's the other thing.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.320
What else do you want me to predict?

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:21.300
Well, I think looking at the schedule here, okay, touchdown fever, I know that this one's hard to do because we don't record the episodes live.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.160
But yesterday was the Bills-Lions Thursday night game.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.220
Cool. What do you think the score was of that one?

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.600
I'm pretty sure that's going to be closer.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.020
Probably more like...

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.200
No, you're setting me up to make a fool of myself, Mike.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:42.660
The Bills are not guaranteed to be good this year.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.060
We're probably not undefeated as this episode airs.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.260
Everything sucks.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.980
It was 31 to 5.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.540
There was one field goal and a safety that we scored.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.800
Nice. So that's a scoreigami.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:00.860
At least we can root for something.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.900
Yeah, exactly. I would like to see, actually, 31 to 5 a scoreigami.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.120
Meanwhile, you guys maybe talk about the game?

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:16.300
Yeah. Well, I don't want to gloss over the hustle, the bustle, the muscle, the chomp, or whatever that is.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.180
Is that a football thing?

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.160
Oh, yeah. What is that?

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.320
It's not. I have no idea what the fuck they're talking about.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.520
I was like, as soon as you said, oh, yeah, I was like, oh, my God, I sound so stupid.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.460
I don't know about the chomp.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.540
Yeah, yeah. Last week, you didn't know about missiles.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.320
He doesn't know about the chomp.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:33.680
I don't know about the chomp.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.260
I'm like, you said yes, I was like, shit, I sound like such a bleep.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.660
I don't know about the chomp in football.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.580
Can you imagine, like, pretending to be an American man and not knowing about the chomp?

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.160
The chain chomp.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.140
What are they talking about then?

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.160
What is that?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.180
I have no idea. Why is that written on the back of the box?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.380
I googled it, and all I got was it's the famous marketing tagline of Touchdown Fever.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.920
The thing I will say about, like, my first impressions of this game, just going by the instruction manual,

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.720
is they expect that you have never heard of American football.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:14.280
The manual is mostly teaching you about the sport of football, like the rules of the sport,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.820
and has very little information about how they sort of video gamify it.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:35.440
It's like a brief introduction to the control scheme and sort of explaining the info screens that happen between plays,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.040
and then the rest of it is just, like, very base level, what does 4th down mean?

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.240
Which I found very endearing.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:50.720
I just wanted to, you know, close the door on Scorigami and just say that four years ago,

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.720
the Cardinals beat the Texans 31-5.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.100
Oh, wow. Shit.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:00.240
Yeah, it has happened, and pretty recently, too.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.980
You know, Sean, thank you for bringing us back to the game.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:08.280
I appreciate everything that you said, and I don't disagree or...

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.460
Or was particularly listening.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:11.900
Yeah, I don't want to correct anything you were saying.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.060
I just wanted to point out that in addition to everything that you were saying about the game,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:23.480
the first thing that I took away from this game after playing it for maybe, like, not even five minutes

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.100
was that I...

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:31.460
This is the first game that maybe made a football field feel long.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.900
Oh, but it feels, like, longer than it should.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.020
Right, longer than it should, no doubt.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.460
Like, it feels like the players are incredibly tiny,

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.620
because I was so surprised that, like, some of my plays,

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.480
which felt like, you know, nice long passes that, like...

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.660
20-yard chunk play here.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.780
Right, right, exactly. And then all of a sudden I see, like, third down.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.480
I'm like, what the hell? I've been getting first down every time.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:56.780
Like, what are they talking about?

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:04.780
It's just dramatically different scale than Tecmo or John Elway or anything else we've played on this system.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:10.200
So, and I'm sorry that John Elway was my second reference for an NES football game,

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:11.500
but, like, you know what I mean.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.740
All the other ones are so zoomed out by comparison

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.000
that I'm just not sure why the players aren't bigger here.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:26.600
The only thing that I can, like, possibly say is that you're playing as, like, toddlers.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:36.160
Because each yard is, like, you could fit two people between each yard mark here.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.040
So I think that's the only thing it could be.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.980
It's like you're playing football for ants.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:48.500
Yeah. Yeah, and they do look, they're kind of got the, like, chibi look to them, too.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:53.080
Sort of reminds me a little bit of, um, and what is the, not Blades of Steel,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.760
the other early hockey game we played?

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.220
It's just like NES hockey.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.660
Yeah. Yeah, ice hockey.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.020
You got, like, a three different style player, whatever.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:07.340
This game overall did that, did what that game did for me for football.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.660
Where, like, this feels a little more, like, arcade-y than some of the other games.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.060
And just, like, yeah, you're smaller.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:20.580
Yes, it does feel like you're, you're going, you're, you're, the, the field feels way longer than it should.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:30.200
But it also just, it just feels, like, a little more accessible and, I don't know, easy to, like, read football NES game, if that makes sense.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.820
And they obviously, they did not get the NFL license here.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:42.040
They, they're just, like, you know, giving you random, um, uh, cities to play as, which happened to also coincide with real NFL team locations.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:48.280
But you're not promised to be playing as, uh, you know, the Jets when you play as New York.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.720
Uh, you're just playing as, uh, you know, blue team.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.840
And, uh, you can tell that the reason that they did that was not because, um, they couldn't afford the license.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:07.080
It's because they're not using NFL, uh, they're not using the NFL rulebook because every player is allowed to grease themselves before they, uh, get on the field.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.620
Because it's, have you ever noticed how many computer players can touch you before they successfully tackle you?

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:18.380
Like, so many times in this game did I think I was, like, oh, that plays over.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.980
And then they just don't successfully tackle me.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.460
It's, like, the only thing I can come to the conclusion of is that we're all greased up.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:31.320
Yeah, there's a few, like, weird little, like, weird little things that, like, I guess make the game more playable but don't really make sense if you look at it.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:36.160
That being one of the other ones that, like, when you have the ball, I guess the weight of the ball slows you down.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.120
Because you are always slower than anyone chasing you.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:41.460
Yeah, it's, it's...

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.240
Well, it is hard to run while holding a ball.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.040
It's also an automatic interception if you throw the ball over somebody.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.480
Um, I know that there are some instances...

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:59.000
Well, these people are infinitely tall. Yeah, I know there's some instances where, like, the game decides that you did throw the ball relatively high and it'll just, like, sort of go over them.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:04.340
But it does not, it's not very good at, uh, showing that.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:17.580
And I think the biggest gripe that I have with this game, or, like, the most confusing thing about it, is that you're ostensibly choosing a play before each snap.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:27.420
It says, like, short pass, long pass, uh, backs, which I guess just means a run play, um, field goal.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.780
Now, if you choose field goal, you will line up for a field goal.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.300
But everything else is just, like, a random play.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.060
Like, there is no...

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.440
Like, the...

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.720
Did you notice this?

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:44.540
That, like, where... Yes, no, yeah, you're just making the decision between a short and long pass, a QB sneak, and a regular running play.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:53.860
Like, I have chosen long pass, and then both of my receivers just sort of hang out at the line of scrimmage, and I don't understand what that's about.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:55.900
Um, it...

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:59.100
And then they're always lined up in random places.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:03.880
There's not any real way to, like, plan this out.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.720
Um... Oh, like, there's no route to see ahead of time.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.240
Yeah, you have no idea what your player's gonna do.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.380
Yeah, exactly. And it'll be counterintuitive like that.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:18.600
And there's, like, it says backs, but sometimes your backs won't even be involved in the play.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.520
Um, and you're still passing it.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:28.100
Now, one thing that they kind of did to offset this is that you can lateral the ball after a forward pass.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.280
Um, and I get that that is useful, but it is...

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:41.380
It's a very strange mechanic to have in there when everything else is kind of, uh, is kind of paired back.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:50.580
Yeah, and also, you know, this whole game seems to be designed around a system where you could just, like, the strategy is just laterals, right?

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:56.200
Just constantly just keep throwing laterals to one another, and that's more effective than any particular forward progress.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:04.640
Yeah. Well, the one thing, another note on just the fact that this isn't the NFL, uh, fun fact, that is, um...

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:11.120
Uh, they have the two-point conversion, and the NFL didn't really have that, and I think until, like, the next year.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.020
So, that's a cute thing.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:20.820
But then you get the halftime show, and then there's obviously some correlation, because I played as Detroit.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:26.880
Um, the halftime show showed, you know, my cheerleaders come out with the mascot of a lion.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:33.560
Um, but I was playing against Pittsburgh, and I think it was just, like, some tiger that came out.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.600
So, I'm not really sure what that was about.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:36.540
Yes, yes, yes.

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.860
The, the... But Miami gets a dolphin.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.000
Miami does get a dolphin. Yeah, and Denver had some kind of horse-looking thing.

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:44.960
Yes. I thought it was a cow at first, but...

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.580
I thought it was a hippo at first, and then I realized, oh, it's Denver, so...

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:54.660
Uh, you know, we were talking about the greased-up players, and that's true of both the...

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.060
Like, I want to just say that, like, I did have...

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.760
The computer did send, like, nine guys against me, and they all successfully missed me.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.100
But that can happen to you as well, because there is no tackle button.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:07.540
Yeah, you just sort of get in the way.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:10.700
You just collide and hope that they fall over.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.180
It's funny, I didn't realize there was no tackle button, because I always just hit the button.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.360
You think you're tackling, right, right.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:16.620
You're probably hitting those buttons.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:21.340
Yeah, and sometimes it would work. We are all the little brother with the unplugged controller.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:29.580
Right. A and B are only used to select either the A or B player that it shows you at the start of the formation.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.960
From there, you're just that person.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:42.080
Which is really infuriating when, like, you have another player that has kind of an angle where you can make a tackle, but you can't switch to him.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:03.600
And I hate to say it, but I mean, like, that's the game.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.040
Like, that's what we're seeing.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:11.740
So I guess now we need to start talking about, like, the presentation part, which we covered, that the field's enormous.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.600
But even, and we covered the halftime show.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:19.840
So I guess, like, you know, I guess we could talk maybe that the teams are always just blue versus pink.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.780
Or that the way that the score is presented.

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:23.900
You can be blue versus gray.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:25.840
Oh, you can be.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.980
Okay, sorry. I thought it was always blue versus pink.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:30.100
Yeah, there's some different colors.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:31.280
There's some gray teams.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:48.520
Yeah. One of the most befuddling decisions is they don't flip the perspective when a team, when, like, you know, the away team takes possession of the ball.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:53.380
It will just now have the offense on top, which is insane to me.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:59.020
I know that there's no actual rule against that, but I feel like there should be.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:02.440
I don't like any, even, like, 10-yard fight.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:03.700
I don't even know if that had that.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:16.900
And then there's sort of a, like, they kind of have this in place to, like, make you think that maybe there are some more spread out formations in this game.

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:21.440
But before each snap, the camera will pan left and right.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:26.880
But the entire team is going to be visible anyway.

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:29.580
So it's kind of unnecessary.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:34.000
And when you're defense, you don't get to pick a formation.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:35.460
Nope. Right? Nope.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:41.300
You just get what you're dealt and you pick whichever player you want to pursue with.

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.300
I always say less choice is better anyway.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:46.640
Yeah, maybe in a mobile game.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.160
No, and you're saying, I'm teasing.

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:58.020
Joking aside, I, like, me as someone who does not care for football video games, American football video games just don't do it for me.

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:03.200
You guys have probably noticed every time, other than this year, when we've got a Long Island Retro Game Fest.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:08.120
And there's always a point when we go to that, what is it, like, some Madden arcade game and, like, the three of us play.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.860
A Blitz. And, like, I just, like, I'm sure you can read it on me.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:13.880
I'm just like, all right, I don't care about this at all.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:15.900
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:17.640
I hate picking plays.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.560
I hate, like, looking through that stuff.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:22.540
And honestly, while I was playing this, I was like, you know what?

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:26.660
